1. RUN → Build Time

RUN commands are executed when the Docker image is being built.

Example:

FROM ubuntu

RUN apt update
RUN apt install -y nginx

When you run:

docker build -t myimage .

Docker executes the RUN commands and saves their results into image layers.

Think:

RUN = Prepare the image

Common uses:

Install packages
Install applications
Copy/configure dependencies
Execute setup commands
2. CMD → Runtime

CMD specifies the default command that runs when a container starts.

Example:

FROM ubuntu

RUN apt update
RUN apt install -y nginx

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

Here:

RUN apt install -y nginx

➡️ happens while building the image.

Then:

CMD ["nginx", "-g", "daemon off;"]

➡️ happens when the container starts.

Easy way to remember 🧠
RUN → BUILD TIME → Creates/updates image
CMD → RUN TIME   → Starts the application/container
Real-life example

Imagine you're preparing a house:

RUN
 ↓
Install doors
Install windows
Install electricity
Install furniture
 ↓
IMAGE READY

Then:

CMD
 ↓
"Start living in the house"
 ↓
CONTAINER RUNNING
Important distinction
RUN	CMD
Build time	Runtime
Creates image layers	Provides default startup command
Used to install/configure	Used to start application
Executes during docker build	Executes when container starts
